module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.a_phantom_composed_alias_formal_admits_a_bare_kernel_value

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }

data a_phantom_composed_alias_formal_admits_a_bare_kernel_value: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "a_phantom_composed_alias_formal_admits_a_bare_kernel_value" as NonEmptyStr,

  receipts: [
    "**a type alias whose right-hand side is a PHANTOM composition carries no inhabitant, so it is transparent to its underlying kernel type at a formal and admits a bare kernel value unchecked -- and the harm is not the admission, it is that the alias READS as a proof and is cited as one.** `std.integer` declares `type UInt8 = Compose<UInt, MachineWidth<8>>`, and `Compose` is `Phantom`: it HAS NO INHABITANT. So a bare `Int` reaches a `UInt8` parameter with nothing checked, and `List<UInt8>` establishes nothing whatever about the range of its members.",

    "THIS IS A NAMING AND READING DEFECT BEFORE IT IS A CHECKING ONE, which is why it belongs in this directory rather than being folded into the checker rows beside it. `UInt8` is a perfectly good name for the CONCEPT of an 8-bit unsigned value; it is a misleading name for a FORMAL, because the reader's whole prior is that a type named for a bounded range bounds it. The corpus already pays for that prior: `std.integer` had to mint a SECOND carrier, `QualifiedOctets`, whose annotation says outright that it 'does not mean a list of octets; it means EVERY MEMBER PASSED uint8_of_int ADMISSION' -- a separate type existing solely to carry the proof the first type's name already implies.",

    "MEASURED, AND THE RECEIPT IS ALREADY IN THE CORPUS rather than produced for this row: `std.integer`'s own annotation records that `test.claim.machine_word_witness` called a `List<UInt8>` formal with `[256, 0, 0]` and that the claim COMPILED AND EXECUTED in the floor receipt at 9ca9d9ab -- 256 crossed a `UInt8` formal with no refusal. That is the class firing on the real acceptance path, in a witness, unnoticed.",

    "HOW IT BITES A READER, 2026-09-22, gunbc#12045, recorded because the wrong conclusion was reached and then caught rather than being hypothesised. Repairing a live site that hands a raw `List<UInt8>` where `std.encoding` `base64_encode` declares `QualifiedOctets`, the author concluded that `List<UInt8>` ALREADY carries the per-member proof elementwise, which would make `QualifiedOctets`-over-`List<Int>` the weaker of the two representations and the whole carrier redundant -- a §3 nickname finding against a type that is in fact load-bearing. The conclusion was reversed only by reading `std.integer` line 47, where the phantom fact is stated. It is a reasonable inference from the name, it is wrong, and NOTHING AT THE USE SITE SAYS SO: the warning is findable only by a reader who already suspects it.",

    "DISTINGUISHING FACTS, against the three neighbours it will be confused with. It is NOT `bounded_natural_arithmetic_evaluated_as_unbounded_int` -- that row is about the OPERATIONS (infix arithmetic on bounded naturals evaluated as 64-bit Int); this row is about the FORMAL admitting an out-of-range value in the first place, and closing either leaves the other open. It is NOT `direct_generic_application_admits_a_distinct_phantom_argument` -- there two DISTINCT phantom identities are confused with each other; here one phantom identity fails to constrain its own underlying kernel type. It is NOT `refined_alias_argument_exempt_from_declared_type_inhabitance` -- that is a `where`-refined alias at a RECORD formal, a refinement that exists and is not consulted; here there is no refinement to consult, because the composition is phantom by construction.",

    "A SECOND CONSUMER MAY FAIL ON THE SAME DECLARATION IN THE OTHER DIRECTION -- SUSPECTED, UNCITED, AND NAMED HERE ONLY SO A READER WHO FINDS ONE DIRECTION KNOWS TO LOOK FOR THE OTHER. The checker asks this declaration 'does this value inhabit it' and answers by admitting everything, which is this row and is measured. The EMITTER asks it 'what target text realizes it'. An emitted crate carrying `extdeps.numeric.base16` -- which imports `UInt8` from `std.integer` and uses `List<UInt8>` in three functions -- was observed failing to build with four E0425 errors, the reported mechanism being that the module's `use` line for `UInt8` is never emitted while the signatures still render the name. THAT IS NOT THIS ROW'S RECEIPT AND IS NOT CITED AS ONE (DESIGN §4d): it is second-hand, it is not reproduced, and the repair in flight removes `UInt8` from `base16` altogether, which removes the SYMPTOM without establishing whether the defect was real or latent. WHERE IT IS BEING SETTLED BY EXECUTION: sharp-bear-756's lane, 'Restore main self-host build: base16 UInt8 import binding'. A reader wanting the emitter direction should go there for a fact, not here.",

    "TWO FIRST READINGS OF THAT DIRECTION WERE WRONG, AND BOTH CORRECTIONS ARE KEPT because they are what stops the next reader repeating them. FIRST: the hypothesis was that the emitter HAS NO RULE FOR LOWERING a phantom `Compose` into any target type. The error text falsifies it -- E0425 is an unresolved NAME, not an unrepresentable type, and the emitter renders `UInt8` in the signatures perfectly well; it fails to IMPORT it. Any claim that a phantom composition cannot be lowered is false and must not be repeated from this row. SECOND: once that was corrected it was tempting to keep a weaker version -- that both directions descend from one under-specified declaration, by way of an emitter stage that skips a phantom alias when computing imports while the signature stage renders it. THAT IS ALSO UNESTABLISHED. What is actually shared between the two is that both involve `UInt8`, which is far weaker than a common cause: a dropped `use` emission is a defect an ordinary non-phantom import could have too, and nothing shows the phantom-ness is load-bearing in it. This row claims only its own direction.",

    "WHERE THE EMITTER DIRECTION BELONGS, WHICH IS NOT HERE AND IS NOT A NEW ROW, and the correction above strengthens rather than weakens this judgement: `gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.accepted_source_emits_uncompilable_target` already carries it at class grain -- its trigger is ACCEPTED-SOURCE-TO-TARGET AGREEMENT for every construction whose emission carries a type or realization obligation, and its population is already several mechanisms. A name rendered but never imported is one more mechanism in THAT row's population. The two directions fail every test for being one class: the invalid state differs (a wrong value admitted vs. a target program that will not build), the harm differs (silent wrongness vs. a loud build failure), the rung differs (below the ladder on interpretation vs. below it at the emission boundary), and the TRIGGERS ARE DISJOINT -- judging a kernel value against `MachineWidth<N>` would not emit a missing `use`, and emitting the `use` would admit 256 exactly as before. Filing it here would fork a class whose trigger already covers it.",

    "RUNG FOUND AT: silent acceptance, which is outside the ladder and forbidden (DESIGN §5), for the admission itself. CEILING: structurally guaranteed. The class is decidable -- `MachineWidth<8>` names the bound in the declaration, so an `Int` literal or value at a `Compose<UInt, MachineWidth<N>>` formal is judgeable from the two resolved heads without any new authority. It is not structurally IMPOSSIBLE, because the out-of-range literal remains authorable in source.",

    "NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER, NAMED AS A CAPABILITY: the declared-type inhabitance judgment reads the width argument of a `Compose<_, MachineWidth<N>>` formal and refuses a kernel value outside that width, SUFFICIENT FOR making the bare-Int-at-UInt8 call a located compiler refusal rather than a runtime abort or a silent pass. That is strictly more than the sole-construction workaround the corpus reaches for today: `QualifiedOctets` closes ONE list-shaped use by minting a second carrier, and every future phantom-composed alias needs its own second carrier until this judgment exists. A SEPARATE, CHEAPER MITIGATION that does not retire this row: an annotation at each phantom-composed alias declaration saying what it does NOT establish, so the reader who forms the natural inference is corrected where they are reading rather than where the authority happens to live.",

    "NOT CLAIMED HERE: how many phantom-composed aliases in the corpus are consumed as if they constrained. `std.integer` declares ten `Compose<Int, MachineWidth<N>>` rows on one axis and `UInt8` is the one with a measured receipt; the others were not probed one at a time, and a census of formals typed by a phantom composition is the honest next measurement.",
  ],

  evidence: [],
}
